<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick I, Duke of Württemberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1557">1557</a>–<a href="/years/1608">1608</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Frederick I of Württemberg-Mömpelgard</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick I, Duke of Württemberg, ruled the County of Montbéliard from 1581 and inherited the Duchy of Württemberg in 1593. A Lutheran prince with wide interests, he employed the architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-schickhardt">Heinrich Schickhardt</a>, who rebuilt much of Montbéliard and founded the new town of Freudenstadt. He strengthened ducal power against the Württemberg estates.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-i-duke-of-wurttemberg.jpg" alt="Frederick I, Duke of Württemberg" width="236" height="332" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_I,_Herzog_von_W%C3%BCrttemberg_(1557-1608).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick was born on 19 August 1557 at Mömpelgard, the present-day Montbéliard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the son of George I of Württemberg-Mömpelgard and Barbara of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-landgrave-of-hesse">Philip I, Landgrave of Hesse</a>. He spent his youth at the Württemberg court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christoph-duke-of-wurttemberg">Christoph, Duke of Württemberg</a>, took charge of his education himself, and from 1571 to 1574 he studied at the later Collegium Illustre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>. As a young prince he travelled to the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, France, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Frederick signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/formula-of-concord">Formula of Concord</a> of 1577 and the Book of Concord of 1580. In 1592, as heir apparent to Württemberg, he visited Windsor and other English cities and repeatedly asked Queen Elizabeth to make him a Knight of the Garter. She admitted him to the order only after he had inherited the duchy, and in a calculated slight he was not told in time to attend the investiture of spring 1597.</p><p>That ceremony was the occasion for Shakespeare's The Merry Wives of Windsor, whose jokes about a German duke who is not expected at Windsor, and about a stolen horse, refer to his visit and his absence; they appear in the First Folio but not in the 1602 Quarto. He was finally invested at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> on 4 October 1603 by a delegation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI and I</a>.</p><p>In 1599 he ordered a new town, Freudenstadt, to be built at the northern end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a>, meant as a new residence for the duchy closer to Mömpelgard than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Frederick died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> on 29 January 1608, aged 50, and his plan of a residence at Freudenstadt never came about. With his wife Sibylla of Anhalt, a daughter of Joachim Ernst, Prince of Anhalt, he had fifteen children. His eldest son Johann Frederick succeeded him, and by an agreement between the brothers on 7 June 1617 younger sons founded the branch lines of Württemberg-Neuenstadt, Württemberg-Mömpelgard and Württemberg-Weiltingen. His son Magnus fell at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-wimpfen">Battle of Wimpfen</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick I, Duke of Württemberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Duke Frederick I</b> ruled it from 1581 and brought in the architect Heinrich Schickhardt.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-montbeliard">County of Montbéliard</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Montbéliard, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.51,6.8&amp;year=1590" aria-label="Montbéliard on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Frederick I, Duke of Württemberg"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5392 6040h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5392 6040h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.51,6.8&amp;year=1590">What was here in 1590? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
